Tetris (tetris.c64)
-------------------
The `gortris` task is a port of the game logic from Wiebo de Wit's
"Tetris for the CBM64 in 6502 Assembly" (`reference/tetris/`).

None of the original's artwork is used: its screens and charset are
char-mode CharPad data, and gortris draws its own field, frame, panel and
text with the kernel's glyph support. Its music is not used either — the
reference embeds a SID tune and plays it from its own raster IRQ, while
gortris plays `music/tetris.mus`, this repository's own three-voice SID
arrangement of the **Korobeiniki** melody -- a traditional Russian folk song,
in the public domain. Its melody and bass line were read from a MIDI made from a
fan piano sheet, credited in the sheet-music entry above; the chords, the
harmony, the waveforms, the envelopes and the form are this repository's own
work. The tune was written, not converted: a `.sid` is a program with
its own player and IRQ, so it cannot be turned into a Sidplayer `.mus` score.
`tools/gen-mus.py` generates it, and it is played through the shared sound
library (`sid.lib`, see the entry above), so the chip stays under the
operating system's control.

Original by Wiebo de Wit (C) 2015.
Website: https://github.com/wiebow/tetris.c64
Ported revision: commit d15f86f6 (2017-01-29) — the game logic only,
re-expressed for GordonOS's bitmap display: the seven tetrominoes and their
rotations, the play-mode state machine and its level rule, full-row detection
and clearing, the game-over animation and the mode machine around it. The
screens and the input are redrawn, the hi-score table is stored in the REU
filesystem, and the hard drop, the music and the quit key are this port's own.
Nothing from the reference is built, imported or linked.

